  {
   "slug": "before-and-after-galley-kitchen",
   "title": "Before & After: The Galley Kitchen",
   "date": "2026-09-29T10:00:00",
   "excerpt": "A narrow 1970s kitchen with orange pine and no daylight became a bright workspace with a long oak counter. Here is how.",
   "categories": [
    "kitchen",
    "before-after"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "oak",
    "small-spaces"
   ],
   "featured_media": "kitchen-after",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Galley kitchens are often the hardest working room in a flat and the least loved. This one was 2.2 metres wide, lined with orange pine and lit by a single tube light.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We kept the layout, which saved a great deal of plumbing, and focused on light, storage and one beautiful material.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:columns {\"align\":\"wide\",\"className\":\"before-after\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide before-after\"><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:kitchen-before}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:kitchen-before}}\" alt=\"The kitchen before, with dark pine units\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:kitchen-before}}\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Before</figcaption></figure><!-- /wp:image --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:kitchen-after}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:kitchen-after}}\" alt=\"The kitchen after, with pale units and an oak counter\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:kitchen-after}}\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">After</figcaption></figure><!-- /wp:image --></div><!-- /wp:column --></div><!-- /wp:columns -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What changed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Flat-front units painted in a soft putty colour</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A continuous oak worktop running the full length</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Open shelves instead of upper cupboards on one wall</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Warm under-cabinet lighting and a linen-shaded pendant</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Removing the upper cupboards on the window side made the biggest difference. Light now reaches the far end of the room for most of the day.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Small rooms reward restraint. Choose one hero material and let it run.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Our lead designer</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "Tomas",
     "content": "We have nearly the same kitchen. This gives me hope!",
     "date": "2026-09-30T19:00:00",
     "email": "tomas@example.com"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "slug": "layering-a-bedroom-in-linen",
   "title": "Layering a Bedroom in Linen",
   "date": "2026-09-12T08:30:00",
   "excerpt": "Crumpled linen, soft wool and warm lamplight. Our guide to a bedroom that feels restful in every season.",
   "categories": [
    "bedroom",
    "styling-tips",
    "shop-the-look"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "linen",
    "neutral"
   ],
   "featured_media": "bedroom",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A restful bedroom is mostly about texture. When the colours are quiet, layers of different fabrics keep the room from feeling flat.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We like to build a bed from the bottom up: a fitted sheet in washed linen, a cotton percale duvet, a waffle blanket folded at the foot and two sizes of pillows.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Our layering formula</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>One crisp layer: cotton percale</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>One soft layer: washed linen</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>One textured layer: waffle or wool</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>One accent: a single cushion in clay or ochre</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:bedroom}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:bedroom}}\" alt=\"A bed layered in linen and wool with a wooden headboard\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:bedroom}}\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Lighting finishes the room. Swap the overhead light for two low lamps with warm bulbs and the room will feel different by tonight.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"shop-look\",\"layout\":{\"type\":\"constrained\"}} --><div class=\"wp-block-group shop-look\"><!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Bedroom</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"shop-list\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list shop-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li><span>Washed linen sheet set<em>Oat</em></span><span class=\"price\">&euro;180</span></li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><span>Waffle blanket<em>Cotton, ecru</em></span><span class=\"price\">&euro;95</span></li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><span>Clay cushion cover<em>Linen</em></span><span class=\"price\">&euro;38</span></li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><span>Ceramic bedside lamp<em>Matte stone</em></span><span class=\"price\">&euro;160</span></li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:group -->",
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "small-bathroom-spa-feeling",
   "title": "A Small Bathroom with a Spa Feeling",
   "date": "2026-08-25T11:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Plaster-pink walls, a round mirror and handmade tiles brought warmth to a windowless bathroom. Before and after inside.",
   "categories": [
    "bathroom",
    "before-after"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "small-spaces",
    "tiles"
   ],
   "featured_media": "bath-after",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Windowless bathrooms can feel clinical. For this project we leaned into warmth: a pink plaster finish, a wall of handmade tiles and lighting at eye level instead of above.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:columns {\"align\":\"wide\",\"className\":\"before-after\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide before-after\"><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:bath-before}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:bath-before}}\" alt=\"The bathroom before, with white tiles and a strip light\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:bath-before}}\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Before</figcaption></figure><!-- /wp:image --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:bath-after}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:bath-after}}\" alt=\"The bathroom after, with pink plaster and handmade tiles\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:bath-after}}\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">After</figcaption></figure><!-- /wp:image --></div><!-- /wp:column --></div><!-- /wp:columns -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The details</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Tadelakt-style plaster, sealed for wet areas</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Handmade zellige tiles behind the basin</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A round mirror with wall lights either side</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A teak stool for towels and plants</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The whole renovation took three weeks. The plumbing stayed where it was, which kept the budget sensible.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Light from the side is kinder than light from above. Especially at seven in the morning.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Studio rule</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "Leah",
     "content": "I did not know plaster could be used in a bathroom. Beautiful.",
     "date": "2026-08-27T09:45:00",
     "email": "leah@example.com"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "slug": "balcony-garden-room",
   "title": "A Balcony That Became a Garden Room",
   "date": "2026-07-18T17:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Four square metres, three pots of olive and one folding bistro set. Small outdoor spaces deserve design too.",
   "categories": [
    "outdoor"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "small-spaces",
    "plants"
   ],
   "featured_media": "balcony",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A balcony is a room, just one with a better ceiling. We treat them the same way as any interior: zones, materials and lighting.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This city balcony faces west, so it gets hot afternoon sun. We chose plants that like it dry and a slatted screen for privacy from the neighbours.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Plant list</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Olive trees in large terracotta pots</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Rosemary and thyme along the railing</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A trailing jasmine on the screen</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:balcony}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:balcony}}\" alt=\"A small balcony with olive trees and a bistro set\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:balcony}}\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Solar string lights and an outdoor rug finish the space. It is now the most-used room in the flat from May to September.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "five-rules-for-styling-shelves",
   "title": "Five Rules for Styling Shelves",
   "date": "2026-10-05T09:30:00",
   "excerpt": "Open shelves can look serene or chaotic. These five simple rules from our studio make the difference.",
   "categories": [
    "styling-tips"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "styling",
    "ceramics"
   ],
   "featured_media": "shelves",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Every client asks us about shelves. They are the place where a home shows its personality, and the place where clutter gathers fastest.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The rules</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Start with books, laid both upright and in small stacks.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Group objects in threes of different heights.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Leave a third of each shelf empty.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Repeat one colour across the whole unit.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Add one living thing: a plant or a branch in a vase.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:shelves}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:shelves}}\" alt=\"Oak shelves with books, ceramics and a plant\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:shelves}}\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Step back and squint. If your eye knows where to rest, you are done. If it jumps around, take one thing away.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:pullquote --><figure class=\"wp-block-pullquote\"><blockquote><p>When in doubt, remove one object.</p></blockquote></figure><!-- /wp:pullquote -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Most of all, use your shelves to tell your story. A pebble from a holiday or a bowl a friend made says more than anything bought to fill a gap.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "Ines",
     "content": "The empty third rule changed my bookcase completely.",
     "date": "2026-10-07T20:15:00",
     "email": "ines@example.com"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "slug": "choosing-a-warm-white-paint",
   "title": "Choosing a Warm White Paint",
   "date": "2026-06-30T10:00:00",
   "excerpt": "There are hundreds of whites. Here is how we choose the right one for north-facing, south-facing and everything in between.",
   "categories": [
    "styling-tips"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "paint",
    "neutral"
   ],
   "featured_media": "paint",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>White is never just white. It takes on the colour of the light, the floor and even the trees outside the window.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>In north-facing rooms, cool light makes many whites look grey. Choose a white with a yellow or red base. In south-facing rooms you can go cleaner and cooler.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How to test</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Paint large swatches, at least A3, on two walls.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Look at them in the morning, afternoon and under lamps.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Hold them next to your floor and largest furniture.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Live with them for three days before deciding.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:paint}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:paint}}\" alt=\"Paint swatches in warm whites and neutrals\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:paint}}\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you cannot decide between two whites, choose the warmer one.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Our colour consultant</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "a-reading-nook-under-the-stairs",
   "title": "A Reading Nook Under the Stairs",
   "date": "2026-08-08T14:00:00",
   "excerpt": "An awkward triangle of wasted space became the favourite spot in the house, with a built-in bench, a wall light and a lot of cushions.",
   "categories": [
    "living-room",
    "projects",
    "shop-the-look"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "small-spaces",
    "oak"
   ],
   "featured_media": "nook",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Under-stair spaces usually end up as storage for coats, shoes and things nobody wants to sort. In this family home we gave the space a better job.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A built-in oak bench with drawers underneath keeps the storage, while a thick cushion and a stack of pillows invite you to stay.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:nook}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:nook}}\" alt=\"A reading nook with a cushioned bench and a wall lamp\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:nook}}\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Make your own</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Measure the lowest headroom and keep the seat beneath it.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Use a wall light so the bench stays free.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Paint the nook a shade darker than the hall for a cocooning feel.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"shop-look\",\"layout\":{\"type\":\"constrained\"}} --><div class=\"wp-block-group shop-look\"><!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Reading nook</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"shop-list\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list shop-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li><span>Bench cushion<em>Made to measure, linen</em></span><span class=\"price\">&euro;240</span></li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><span>Brass wall light<em>With switch</em></span><span class=\"price\">&euro;130</span></li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><span>Wool throw<em>Ochre</em></span><span class=\"price\">&euro;110</span></li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:group -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The whole project took a carpenter four days. It is now where the children read after school and where their parents hide with a coffee on Sunday mornings.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "Ben",
     "content": "Our kids would never leave this spot.",
     "date": "2026-08-10T16:40:00",
     "email": "ben@example.com"
    }
   ]
  }
